  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 137 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nFor the vast majority of the extend period, model agreement see\nthe upper-level high pressure system stay situated across the\nWestern United States. For the Panhandles, the positioning of the\nhigh puts us in a sort of mixed bag situation where we stay hot\nand relatively dry, but have the potential see storms move in from\nthe Southern Front Range. As for this weekend, the drier portion\nof the scenario looks to be more prominent as low-level southerly\nflow looks to bring in drier air that will also see the more\nwidespread triple-digit temperatures for both afternoon. This flow\nwill also lead to breezier conditions at the surface, which can\nthen lead to some elevated fire weather concerns as well. However\nmoving into next, week does see the chances of storms once again\nacross the Northwestern Panhandles. These chances do look to\nfurther grow towards the end of next week, with many models\nactually seeing the high start to weaken and become disorganized\nacross the Southern Untied States. Either way, look for\ntemperature to keep hot with 90s to triple-digits likely every\nday.",
